  ],
  "ed25519": {
    "_comment": "Deterministic kernel key - the SAME RFC 8032 seed as mint_v1.json and chain_v1.json. Every document in every case is signed by this ONE key: these chains are single-trust-domain, so no consent attestation is required or consulted and expiry is the only variable.",
    "kernel_key_thumbprint": "urn:ietf:params:oauth:jwk-thumbprint:sha-256:1IG2tMH7J2wbJZnOf8LJzQitKf7LMvoAElsuDMVM54Y",
    "kernel_private_key_seed_hex": "000102030405060708090a0b0c0d0e0f101112131415161718191a1b1c1d1e1f",
    "kernel_public_key_hex": "03a107bff3ce10be1d70dd18e74bc09967e4d6309ba50d5f1ddc8664125531b8"
  },
  "expiry_containment": [
    {
      "child_expires_at": "2026-06-05T11:00:00Z",
      "expected": "permitted",
      "name": "control: a child ending before its parent is contained",
      "parent_expires_at": "2026-06-05T12:00:00Z",
      "why": "THE POSITIVE CONTROL on this surface. Without it, an implementation whose predicate returns false unconditionally passes every negative below."
    },
    {
      "child_expires_at": "2026-06-05T12:00:00Z",
      "expected": "permitted",
      "name": "equality is contained",
      "parent_expires_at": "2026-06-05T12:00:00Z",
      "why": "`child <= parent`. The mint MUST NOT refuse a delegation that ends exactly when its parent does."
    },
    {
      "child_expires_at": "2026-06-05T12:00:01Z",
      "expected": "refused",
      "name": "one second later is refused",
      "parent_expires_at": "2026-06-05T12:00:00Z",
      "why": "The smallest representable overrun under the E.6 whole-second profile. A rule that only fires on a gross overrun would pass a suite of hours-long cases and still let the continuous form of the defect through - a child minted seconds after its parent under one TTL outlives it by exactly the delegation delay."
    },
    {
      "child_expires_at": "2026-06-05T12:00:00Z",
      "expected": "permitted",
      "name": "the comparison is by INSTANT, not by string",
      "parent_expires_at": "2026-06-05T13:00:00+01:00",
      "why": "The same instant, written two ways. An implementation comparing the timestamps as text reads '2026-06-05T12:00:00Z' > '2026-06-05T13:00:00+01:00' as false only by luck of the alphabet, and the next offset form breaks it. ADR-0006: a verifier canonicalizes the values it was PRESENTED, and a presented timestamp may be in any valid RFC 3339 form, so the comparison must parse."
    },
    {
      "child_expires_at": "2026-06-05T13:00:01+01:00",
      "expected": "refused",
      "name": "an offset form that really is later is refused",
      "parent_expires_at": "2026-06-05T12:00:00Z",
      "why": "The negative twin of the case above. Without it, an implementation could pass the previous case by parsing nothing and returning `permitted` whenever the forms differ."
    },
    {
      "child_expires_at": "2026-06-05T11:00:00Z",
      "expected": "refused",
      "name": "an unreadable parent expiry is refused",
      "parent_expires_at": "not a timestamp",
      "why": "FAIL CLOSED, and the same rule verdict_v1 states for standing: an expiry that cannot be read is not an expiry that can be shown to contain anything. A rule that cannot be evaluated must never report that it passed."
    },
    {
      "child_expires_at": "2026-06-05T11:00:00Z",
      "expected": "refused",
      "name": "an absent parent expiry is refused",
      "parent_expires_at": null,
      "why": "Absence and unreadability fail the same way. A missing member is not a permissive one."
    },
    {
      "child_expires_at": null,
      "expected": "refused",
      "name": "an absent child expiry is refused",
      "parent_expires_at": "2026-06-05T12:00:00Z",
      "why": "The fail-closed rule is symmetric. A child whose own expiry cannot be read cannot be shown to end before its parent."
    },
    {
      "child_expires_at": "2026-06-05T11:00:00Z",
      "expected": "refused",
      "name": "a naive timestamp is not RFC 3339 and is refused",
      "parent_expires_at": "2026-06-05T12:00:00",
      "why": "RFC 3339 requires an offset. Guessing UTC would invent a value the signer never wrote - the same rule the standing parser already applies."
    }
  ],
  "mint_rule": {
    "an_expired_parent_is_the_one_case_a_clamp_cannot_answer": "The ceiling would be in the past, so the child would be issued already expired. That is refused rather than issued, BEFORE the proof-of-possession, so the refusal burns no nonce and a caller that renews the parent and retries is not denied for the wrong reason. An unreadable parent expiry is expired, and is refused by the same rule.",
    "clamp_not_refuse_and_why": "Decided in Session 240, after measuring the alternative. Refusing an over-long child instead of clamping it makes delegation impossible for any issuer with a fixed TTL: `expires = now + ttl` is re-evaluated at every mint, so a child minted one second after its parent outlives it by one second and is refused. Measured against the reference mint with one 24-hour issuer: delegation succeeded at a 0.0s delay and was refused at 1.1s. A rule whose correct implementation cannot delegate is not a rule anyone will keep.",
    "roots_are_not_clamped": "A root has no parent to be contained by, so its TTL is the issuer's alone. A clamp applied indiscriminately would silently shorten every root mint.",
    "statement": "At mint, `mint_child` MUST bound the child's `expires_at` by the authenticated parent's: the child is issued with the EARLIER of the issuer's own TTL and the parent's expiry. A delegation from a parent that has ALREADY expired MUST be refused with the same unauthorized-delegation error the other containment refusals raise, and the refusal MUST name the parent's expiry.",
    "the_ceiling_is_an_instruction_the_mint_still_checks": "The issuer is a seam a deployment supplies. An issuer that ignores the ceiling - one written before this rule, or one that gets it wrong - would emit a child outliving its parent that nothing downstream could distinguish from a legitimate one. So the mint applies the SAME matcher to the document actually issued and withholds it if it fails. A cap the issuer may ignore is not a guarantee."
  },
  "rule": {
    "an_unreadable_expiry_contains_nothing": "An absent or unparseable `expires_at` on EITHER side is `refused`, never `permitted`. This is the same fail-closed posture verdict_v1 states for standing, where an unreadable expiry makes a document expired rather than unparseable.",
    "containment_is_not_equality_of_ttl": "The rule bounds the child's EXPIRY by the parent's, not the child's TTL by the parent's. Two documents minted minutes apart under one 24-hour policy have identical TTLs and the later one still outlives the earlier. Comparing TTLs would report containment for exactly the continuous case vaid#79 describes.",
    "controls_are_load_bearing": "Both surfaces carry positive controls. An implementation that refuses everything satisfies every negative case here and no positive one. A checker that drops the controls has disarmed the vector without changing a single negative.",
    "distance_not_a_clock": "Live documents expire nine centuries hence and lapsed ones two decades past, so no verdict here changes with the calendar. The stated `verification_instant` makes each case reproducible; the distance makes it robust even to an implementation that ignores the instant on the standing predicate (BACKLOG B9).",
    "equality_is_contained": "`child <= parent`. A child ending at the same instant as its parent holds authority for no instant in which its parent holds none.",
    "lapse_is_checked_over_ANCESTORS_only": "`expired` is returned when a document ABOVE the leaf has passed its own `expires_at` at the verification instant. The leaf's own standing - expiry and revocation - is not consulted; that is vaid#76, open. The boundary is pinned by a case so that all three implementations draw it in the same place, NOT because it is where the line belongs.",
    "order_of_faults_is_normative": "Where a chain breaks more than one rule, the FIRST fault found is the one reported, and the order is: authenticity, then assembly, then ANCESTOR LAPSE, then hop containment (tenant, scope, capabilities, expiry), then cross-key consent. Three verifiers that reject the same chain for three different reasons agree on every boolean and disagree about what happened - the divergence class verdict_v1 exists to police, applied here.",
    "statement": "A conforming implementation MUST, for each `expiry_containment` case, evaluate whether a child whose `expires_at` is `child_expires_at` is contained by a parent whose `expires_at` is `parent_expires_at`, and return `expected`. It MUST, for each `chain` case, verify the leaf (the last entry) against the other entries presented as a detached bundle, AT the case's `verification_instant`, and return `expected_verification`."
